WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.656
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.656 --> 00:00:05.030
RAJIN SHETH: OK, great.

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:06.700
Thank you all for coming.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:07.700
We really appreciate it.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.880
My name is Rajin Sheth,
and I'm a director

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.400
of product management at
Google responsible for Chrome

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.250
OS, and Android, and
enterprise, and education.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.325
And we're here to
talk to you about what

00:00:18.325 --> 00:00:20.470
we're calling connected retail.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:23.140
And how we think we
can, with a combination

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.020
of devices and the
Cloud, really transform

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.110
the retail experience.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.276
And so I'm going to tell
you a little bit about what

00:00:29.276 --> 00:00:29.810
we're doing.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.590
But then I'm excited to then
hand it off to Greg Chambers

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:37.150
from Coca Cola to actually give
you a real life example of how

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.940
this is taking place and
some of the amazing work

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.270
that Coca Cola is doing using
the combination of Chrome

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:47.980
devices and Google Cloud
in interesting ways.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:53.380
So to start off with, the
concept of the workspace

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:54.760
has really changed.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.850
If you think about all
of us, say 10 years ago,

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.490
we all had a laptop.

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:03.760
For most of us that was
probably the only device we had.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.130
And so all of our content
was on that laptop.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.380
In many cases that was
probably the only device

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.420
we had that was connected
to the internet.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:15.520
And obviously that
changed a bit over time.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.530
But really everything
boiled down to that.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:20.230
We all probably went into
offices, and many of us

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:21.550
still do.

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:24.610
But that was the
way we all worked.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.800
And if you look at the
way people are working now

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.900
and the way work
spaces are oriented,

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.447
it's changing dramatically.

00:01:30.447 --> 00:01:32.530
And you know, this stat
was really amazing to me--

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.680
that 72% of the US
workforce will work

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.890
from a mobile location by 2020.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.590
If you look at the number
of connected devices,

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:48.340
it's expected to expand to
100 billion devices by 2025.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.740
And it just seems like
an unbelievable number

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.960
until you start to think
about all the things

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.950
that are now starting to get
connected to the internet.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.020
You look at then devices
that we can't even

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.290
think of right now that will
affect us down the line.

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:04.990
Things like wearables that are
just kind of at their infancy

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.092
right now that will start
to grow bigger and bigger.

00:02:08.092 --> 00:02:09.550
Things like the
internet of things.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.130
And you think about the
effect that that can then

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.304
have on the workplace itself.

00:02:14.304 --> 00:02:15.970
And that's a lot of
what we want to talk

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.610
about today is, what does
that workplace actually

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:23.760
look like down the line?

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.720
So one of the opportunities
it presents and also

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.750
challenges it presents
is that we can

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.210
become a data-centric world.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:36.240
And businesses can
become data-centric,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.220
and really must
become data-centric,

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.280
to continue to have a
competitive advantage.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.890
And what I mean by
that is that you

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.120
should be able to gather
data, understand that data,

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.990
and then make decisions on
that data very, very quickly.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:55.500
And businesses have obviously
done this for decades,

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.120
and for centuries, really.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.020
But the pace at which this is
happening has grown quite a bit

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.330
just because of the
technology that's out there.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.580
And some of the things that
we're talking to you about

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.040
today is, then how do we
actually take that and apply

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.500
it, especially to retail?

00:03:12.755 --> 00:03:16.260
So we're introducing a
concept called The Connected

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.260
Workspaces.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:20.530
And this is really something
where we're bringing together

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:24.700
a lot of the tools that we
have with Chrome, with Android,

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.130
with Google Cloud, with
things like DoubleClick,

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.980
for example, as well, too,
to really have it such

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:31.690
that you can
instrument a workspace

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.760
and make it even better.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.620
And there are really
three parts of this.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.320
How do you get the workspace
connected in the right way?

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.260
And when I say that
I don't just mean

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.450
how do you get your
knowledge workers connected?

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:46.270
It's more how can you get
every worker connected?

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.030
And beyond that, how can you get
not just your workers but also

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:52.060
your customers connected when
they walk into that space?

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:55.480
And beyond that, how can you get
the space itself and everything

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.730
in that space connected?

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:01.220
By doing so you can start
to gather a lot of data.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.700
This second part is how can
you be smart about that?

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.310
How can you gather that data and
actually make smart decisions

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:07.160
about that?

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.540
And the third part is, how
do you do this securely?

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:12.220
When you have many
more endpoints

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:14.650
and you have all this
data, how can you actually

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:17.089
make it secure?

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.370
So we think that
this feeds itself

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:22.580
into a virtuous cycle,
which is, the first part is

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:23.780
you need to instrument.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:27.770
You need to instrument
your workspace.

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:31.520
And that means having places
that you're getting data from.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.820
In many cases data is
not being captured.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.230
So how do you systematically
get that data?

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:38.420
The second part is that
you need to analyze this.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:39.890
You need to draw
better insights.

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:42.200
And this is where a lot
of the cloud-based tools

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.030
can really come in place.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:47.180
And the third part is, based
on that, you need to improve.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:51.470
And the more that you do this,
the more data that you can then

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:55.340
gather, and it makes your
business better and better,

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.950
because you can then start
to test hypotheses very, very

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:01.320
quickly and then
iterate on those.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.660
So we think that in
those three areas really

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.700
there's sets of technologies
that are very critical.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:10.640
In instrumenting, this is where
devices really come into play.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.850
How can you get
computing to more places

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:15.740
within your enterprise?

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:18.680
How can you, for example, get
mobile devices out farther?

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.680
How can you get larger
computing devices out farther?

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.884
How can you get IOT and sensors
out to different places?

00:05:24.884 --> 00:05:27.050
The second part of this is,
once you have that data,

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:30.090
how can you bring
that into the cloud.

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:33.140
And this is where things
like Google Cloud and data

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.360
warehouses really
come into play.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.250
But then also machine
learning really

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:40.050
comes into play here, as
well, too, which is, great.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.150
You got that data.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:43.220
But then how do you
actually make sense of it?

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:45.860
And how do you make decisions
and make predictions

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:48.150
based on the data
that you have there?

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:50.300
Decisions and predictions
that you probably

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:53.450
would not have made had you just
had a human look at that data

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:54.204
as well.

00:05:54.204 --> 00:05:56.120
And then the third part
is then based on that.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:57.380
How do you improve?

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:59.600
And also, can you
figure out ways by which

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:02.930
you can improve the experience,
and improve your stores,

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:05.750
and improve your
businesses without having

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:08.560
to have a lot of
intervention, or a lot

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:12.140
of manual intervention,
in place there too?

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:15.470
So we believe that with Google
we have all of these tools

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:19.710
that we can provide that
make this especially great.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:23.750
And so starting on one side
we have Android and Chrome OS.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.490
And with those two
operating systems,

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:28.490
you have not only
operating systems

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:32.180
that have been designed from the
ground up in the connected age,

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:33.710
but you also have
operating systems

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:36.980
that can stretch to many,
many different form factors.

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:38.900
With Chrome we
started with laptops.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:42.020
But now we're extending to
everything from digital science

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:45.500
to kiosks to a variety
of different other form

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:49.400
factors that address many,
many different use cases.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.040
With Android, we started
with smartphones.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:54.380
But we're now extending
to the internet of things,

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.590
and getting out to more and more
form factors that way, as well,

00:06:57.590 --> 00:06:58.190
too.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:00.290
By having these
out there you can

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:02.437
start to bring in
data from more places.

00:07:02.437 --> 00:07:04.520
The other thing that's
there is that you can start

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:06.950
to get devices out to everyone.

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:09.680
So it's no longer
about having a $700

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.390
phone in the hands of every
individual, or a $1,500 laptop.

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:16.340
You can now start
to get computing

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:18.410
into the hands of every
employee out there,

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:21.110
and every customer that
walks into the store.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:25.610
The second part of this is
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:27.560
And especially with
the tools that we

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:30.500
have around data, analytics,
and machine learning,

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:32.990
you can do a variety of
things there to then make

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:34.820
sense of the data that's there.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:37.870
And then we have the
connected productivity tools.

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:42.020
G Suite, to be able to have
humans understand that data,

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:44.450
take a look at that data, and
be able to make sense of it

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:45.980
as well, too.

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:47.780
We have a whole
host of applications

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:49.970
that we're then trying
to integrate into this

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:51.320
that will make this better.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:54.170
Everything from, for example,
Firebase, which lets you

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:56.677
build mobile applications
in a more efficient manner.

00:07:56.677 --> 00:07:59.010
Google Play to be able to
distribute those applications.

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:01.850
DoubleClick to be able
to push content out

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:05.550
to intelligent screens
that you have out there.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.070
So the whole package
really kind of

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:09.530
comes together to
be able to give you

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:12.710
the optimal connected workspace.

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:17.420
So one of the places that this
really applies well is retail.

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:20.070
And so I think about this.

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:22.280
How many of you enjoy
going into a store?

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:25.710
I definitely do.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:27.890
So I order a lot
of stuff online.

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.930
But I love going to the
store for a few reasons.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:34.789
One of the reasons I like it
is because if I don't know what

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:36.950
I want I can browse, and
I can find things that

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:39.580
might be of interest to me.

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:42.500
If I do know what I want I
can go and experience that.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:43.250
And I can see it.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:43.875
I can touch it.

00:08:43.875 --> 00:08:44.560
I can feel it.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.300
If it's a piece of
clothing I can try it on.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:49.130
If it's a shoe I
can find my size.

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:51.680
I can do things
like that that only

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.150
I can do if I physically
experience product.

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.540
There's the expertise that
I get by talking to somebody

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:58.456
and having a conversation
with somebody that's

00:08:58.456 --> 00:09:00.510
an expert in that
particular area.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:03.860
So for example, when I went
and bought running shoes

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:05.900
I was able to talk
to an expert that

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:09.980
then cured much of the ailments
that I had with knee problems

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:11.989
by giving me the right shoe.

00:09:11.989 --> 00:09:14.030
And I wouldn't have been
able to do that unless I

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:15.870
talked to an expert
and they'd seen

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:18.002
see me actually run and walk.

00:09:18.002 --> 00:09:20.210
And then finally, you get
the immediate gratification

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:23.090
of getting that item right away.

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:24.980
Now the problem with
a store is a lot

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:29.780
of stores you walk into
when you walk in you have

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:31.670
no idea where you're going.

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:34.190
A lot of the things that
are appealing about online,

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:36.560
where you go to
someplace, you get content

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:37.910
that's tailored towards you.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:39.710
You can find things
very, very quickly.

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:42.320
You can find other
information, reviews,

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:45.530
things like that you can't
usually find in a store.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:47.300
So how can we make
it such that you

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:49.550
can get that kind of
immersive experience?

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:51.210
And we can do it in a few ways.

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:53.210
One is we need to
connect the store.

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:55.310
But then we can
empower the employees

00:09:55.310 --> 00:09:56.690
to know more, as well.

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:59.480
And be able to get more
information out to them

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:02.390
so that they're trained on
the latest and greatest,

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:04.490
and do that through
digital tools.

00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:07.370
We can augment that shopping
by presenting offers,

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:09.830
by presenting personalized
information that

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:12.050
might be relevant to the
shopper that is there

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:13.490
at that particular time.

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:15.150
We can provide
immersive experiences.

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:18.350
So for example, make it so that
you can experience something

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:20.120
that you wouldn't
experience in the store

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:23.010
but you would experience about
the product somewhere else.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:24.740
And then we can
streamline the services

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:28.130
to make it so that you can
check out very, very quickly.

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:32.919
And all of that should be put
back into the management system

00:10:32.919 --> 00:10:34.460
so that you can
manage all this well,

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:37.607
and put back into the supply
chain so you're able to then

00:10:37.607 --> 00:10:40.190
make better decisions about what
you put in the store and what

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:41.960
you don't.

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:43.235
So some of the example--

00:10:43.235 --> 00:10:45.110
I'm going to go through
a few of the examples

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.990
that we're now thinking
about, in terms of technology,

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:49.430
that addresses this.

00:10:49.430 --> 00:10:51.839
Some of which are things
that are there and ready now,

00:10:51.839 --> 00:10:53.630
and some of the things
which we're starting

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:55.140
to experiment with, as well.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:58.430
And we're looking to work with
customers to experiment with.

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:00.560
So one of them that's, of
course, there right now,

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:01.940
is mobile-enabled staff.

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:03.650
And with Android
you have the ability

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:06.890
to really get mobile
devices to more people.

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:14.102
And by doing that you make the
sales associates more informed

00:11:14.102 --> 00:11:15.560
when they're talking
to a customer.

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:17.990
They can look up information
right then and there,

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:20.810
and they can answer
questions right away.

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:23.420
They can engage
with the customers

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:25.580
in a very, very
different way if they

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:28.400
have that at their disposal.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:31.460
And then also, once
they have a sale

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:34.820
they can actually use
things like a mobile POS

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:38.060
to be able to actually make
that sale right then and there,

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:39.290
as well, too.

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:45.404
And so it can help drive sales
in a very interesting way.

00:11:45.404 --> 00:11:47.320
The second thing which
we've seen a lot about,

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:49.894
and I'm going to let Greg
talk a lot more about this,

00:11:49.894 --> 00:11:52.060
because Coca Cola is doing
some amazing things here,

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:54.610
is with smart signs.

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:57.580
You think about a paper
sign, and what you

00:11:57.580 --> 00:12:00.430
can do with a paper sign.

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:03.130
We're now getting to the
point where it's actually

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:06.190
less expensive to
put up a digital sign

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:08.170
than it is to put
up a paper sign.

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:11.200
But moreover, you can do
many more things with that.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:12.460
You can try different things.

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:14.170
You can push
information centrally.

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:16.870
So you can do things like
real-time AB testing.

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:19.210
You could start to
encourage more interaction.

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:21.340
You can start to modify
the content based

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:24.370
on who's looking at the
sign, and tailor that content

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:26.730
to whoever happens to be there.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:30.370
Things that you can only
do if that sign is digital.

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:35.450
And you can actually then see
an uplift in sales as a result.

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:37.070
The next thing is
engagement tracking.

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:39.710
So let's say somebody is
engaging with the product.

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:42.500
How do you know that people
are stopping and looking

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:43.430
at a product?

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:45.020
Or are they just
walking right by

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:47.060
and they're just not
interested in this?

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:49.100
And so you can
start to use sensors

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:53.860
to get anonymized data
to be able to see,

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:55.220
are people stopping?

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:56.240
Are people not stopping?

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:57.740
Are they engaging
with the product?

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:00.830
And be able to then
adjust your product set

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:03.490
and adjust where you
put things in the store

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:05.220
as a result of
that, as well, too.

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:07.160
And it allows smart
screens, as well,

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:09.710
to display relevant
product information

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:12.230
as a result of the data
you're having in real time.

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:13.590
So you can get that data.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:14.590
That You can analyze it.

00:13:14.590 --> 00:13:16.550
And you can push the
right information out

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:20.920
to the smart signs
as a result of this.

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:22.390
The next one is virtual reality.

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:23.890
And so for example,
a lot of stores

00:13:23.890 --> 00:13:26.620
sell things that you
can't necessarily

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:28.210
experience in the store.

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:30.070
So for example, you
may go into a store

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:32.320
that sells vacation packages.

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:34.330
How do you sell somebody
on a vacation package

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:36.370
when you just have
a set of brochures?

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:38.140
A better way to
do this is if you

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:39.730
can use things like
virtual reality

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:42.310
to have them experience
a place like Hawaii,

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:45.490
or the Bahamas, or
Europe, and then

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:47.260
be able to judge for
themselves, is that

00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:48.910
a place that they want to go?

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:50.699
In addition to
that, you can take

00:13:50.699 --> 00:13:52.240
products that are
in the store and be

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:54.760
able to have people
experience that in context.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:56.410
So for example, a surfboard.

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:58.570
How does that surfboard work?

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:01.189
And does it work the
way that you wanted to?

00:14:01.189 --> 00:14:02.980
Make it so that you're
able to actually see

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:07.800
that in the context before
you buy it in the store.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:10.510
And so for example,
one of the places

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:12.520
we see this used a
lot is with furniture.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:14.350
Figuring out how does
a piece of furniture

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:18.596
look in a particular room and
how the different colors look

00:14:18.596 --> 00:14:19.720
with different backgrounds.

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:20.428
Things like that.

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:26.910
Another area-- this
is more experimental,

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:29.270
but we think can be very
powerful in the future--

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:31.100
is indoor location tracking.

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:34.520
And so we're experimenting with
the same kinds of technologies

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:37.130
that we have in Google
self-driving cars,

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:40.910
called LIDAR, to be able
to actually figure out

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:43.610
the flow of people
throughout a store.

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:46.310
So by doing this you can
actually take a look at a store

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:48.980
and be able to see where
are people actually going?

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:50.690
And in aggregate,
where are people going?

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:52.315
So you may end up
finding, for example,

00:14:52.315 --> 00:14:54.620
there's an aisle that
nobody ever goes to.

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:56.330
And you could
start to then adapt

00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:58.130
the store to make that better.

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:01.790
Or put products in that you
want to sell in the places

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:04.460
where people do end up going.

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:06.350
And then of course,
then taking this data,

00:15:06.350 --> 00:15:09.680
then you can start to push
more information back out

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:12.991
to the store and adjust things
real time, as well, too.

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:19.720
And augmented reality is
one of the other areas

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:20.830
we're really exploring.

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:22.990
And again, this
really makes it so

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:27.040
that you can take something
and actually put it in context.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:30.730
How do you look in a
particular sweater,

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:33.970
for example, or in a
particular pair of jeans,

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:37.604
or how does a piece of furniture
look in a particular place?

00:15:37.604 --> 00:15:39.520
This, we think can be
really, really powerful.

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:41.514
As much as virtual
reality is powerful,

00:15:41.514 --> 00:15:43.180
this is a very, very
powerful because it

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:48.800
puts new products in context in
the places that you want them.

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:50.930
And then last but
not least is the idea

00:15:50.930 --> 00:15:53.510
of smart instrumented products.

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:57.320
How do you actually make it such
that the products themselves

00:15:57.320 --> 00:16:00.304
are passing information back
to this connected network?

00:16:00.304 --> 00:16:01.970
And so you know, for
example, are people

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:03.407
picking up the product?

00:16:03.407 --> 00:16:05.240
You know, for example,
where a product might

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:07.850
have gone within the store.

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:12.320
And then be able to display
contextual information based

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:15.200
on, for example, a product
that somebody might be holding

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:16.710
at that particular time.

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:19.400
So many of these things
are possible now.

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:22.640
Many of these things
are things that

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:25.100
are going to be
possible down the line.

00:16:25.100 --> 00:16:30.410
But we really believe that this
is where Google can really help

00:16:30.410 --> 00:16:32.470
with the products that we have.

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:36.360
One of the ones we're
working on quite a bit here,

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.840
and we're starting to
see customers do, is--

00:16:38.840 --> 00:16:42.292
you've heard much about the
Vision API that Google has.

00:16:42.292 --> 00:16:44.750
And you're hearing about what
we're doing with smart signs.

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:46.760
What if you connect
the two together?

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:49.250
You can actually
have a sign where

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:52.809
you're able to understand the
sentiment of the people that

00:16:52.809 --> 00:16:53.600
are in front of it.

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:55.780
When they look at that
sign, are they happy?

00:16:55.780 --> 00:16:57.620
Are they confused?

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:00.890
And based on that you
can then push content.

00:17:00.890 --> 00:17:04.170
You can also then start to do
things like AB testing as well,

00:17:04.170 --> 00:17:07.700
too, with this, to be able to
see whether people really like

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:09.810
what they're seeing
or whether they don't.

00:17:09.810 --> 00:17:11.599
And so you think
about things like,

00:17:11.599 --> 00:17:14.150
that you can really
get to kind of a deeper

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:16.119
level of granularity, at
a deeper level of data

00:17:16.119 --> 00:17:19.940
than was ever possible before.

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:22.210
So I'm going to
hand it off to Greg.

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:26.050
Greg is a group director
at Coca Cola, who's

00:17:26.050 --> 00:17:30.190
been working on trying to
figure out how to utilize

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:31.720
this technology with Coca Cola.

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:34.990
And the thing that's amazing
about Coca Cola, that all of us

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:36.520
know what Coca Cola does.

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:40.930
But they've put a lot of time,
and effort, and expertise

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:46.360
into figuring out how best to
position products in the store,

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:49.450
and how best to actually sell
their products in the store.

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:53.020
And so they've been working
with [? Krumm ?] Digital

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:55.720
Signs and DoubleClick
to really optimize this.

00:17:55.720 --> 00:17:57.205
So I'll hand it off to Greg.

00:17:57.205 --> 00:18:00.390
GREG CHAMBERS: Thank
you so much, sir.

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:02.250
Like he said, I'm
from Coca Cola.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:03.300
My name is Greg Chambers.

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:06.270
I'm a group director of digital
innovation for Coca Cola, which

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:08.760
basically means I'm
in charge of driving

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:13.080
change at a 130-year-old
conservative southern company.

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:15.190
Not the most fun
thing to do sometimes.

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:18.120
But ultimately, I'm a guy
who's spent most of my career

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:19.182
in the startup community.

00:18:19.182 --> 00:18:20.640
And now I work for
a company that's

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:24.250
130 years old that has
this amazing history to it.

00:18:24.250 --> 00:18:26.250
And every single day
that I work at Coke

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:28.260
I learn some new
piece of history.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:31.140
And one of the biggest things
that we see with our history

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:33.540
is the history of
signage, right?

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:34.790
I grew up in Atlanta.

00:18:34.790 --> 00:18:36.480
And there's one thing that
you knew about in Atlanta.

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:38.438
You knew how to find a
good barbecue restaurant

00:18:38.438 --> 00:18:39.810
if you look for the Coke sign.

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:43.950
And signage is really just
built into the DNA of Coca Cola.

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:46.890
So we took this as
an amazing challenge

00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:48.840
to be able to
restart a tradition

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:53.880
at a 130-year-old company, and
do it within a digital realm.

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:56.400
Now, it took a lot of work.

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:59.100
We started this journey
about two years ago.

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:02.970
Coca Cola approached Google
with a radical proposition

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:05.236
on how to do
something different.

00:19:05.236 --> 00:19:07.110
But the first thing that
we started out doing

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:09.660
was immediately going
to rapid iteration.

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:13.200
So in spring in 2015 we
started this journey.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:16.830
By fall of 2015 we had
the first prototype.

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:20.340
And I will be clear about
the first prototype.

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:23.650
There was actually a
picture of the alpha there,

00:19:23.650 --> 00:19:25.292
which you don't see
on there because

00:19:25.292 --> 00:19:27.000
of the lovely positioning
of the cameras.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.100
The duct tape that is
actually holding that screen

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:31.241
onto that cooler.

00:19:31.241 --> 00:19:32.990
We're a 130-year-old
company, but we still

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:35.480
know how to duct tape
stuff in the South.

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:37.250
Moving down the
road, we moved down

00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:38.780
into several
different iterations

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:40.946
of this, different things
that we were trying to do.

00:19:40.946 --> 00:19:43.280
The first alpha that we
produced out in the marketplace

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:45.994
really didn't do much
but play a video, right?

00:19:45.994 --> 00:19:47.660
And it played the
same video that you've

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:51.470
seen 150 million times
every single day.

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:53.285
A video of a Coke pouring.

00:19:53.285 --> 00:19:56.330
The kind of content that
we don't even see anymore.

00:19:56.330 --> 00:19:57.590
It fades into the background.

00:19:57.590 --> 00:19:59.440
We've seen it so many times.

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:01.940
And don't get me wrong, I love
my company, I love Coca Cola,

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:04.070
and I love the
content we produce.

00:20:04.070 --> 00:20:06.440
We moved down into the beta
prototype that we produced.

00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:08.459
We started getting
interactive with it.

00:20:08.459 --> 00:20:10.250
And we'll talk more
about that in a second.

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:12.535
But this is what we
call an OBBO rack.

00:20:12.535 --> 00:20:15.000
Or an Orientation-Based
Bundle Offer.

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:17.030
That's fancy marketing
speak for selling

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:19.010
Coke with something else.

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:20.780
In this case, Mission Foods.

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:23.450
This was a partnership
in-market with Mission Foods.

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:25.332
Where you walked up
to this rack and you

00:20:25.332 --> 00:20:27.040
were able to see a
video of Coke pouring.

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:29.450
And you were able to
get a taco recipe.

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:32.540
And then seamlessly from that it
would broadcast the ingredients

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:35.784
for your taco that you
picked to your phone

00:20:35.784 --> 00:20:37.700
and show you the location
throughout the store

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:39.890
that you were able to get them.

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:43.730
The final thing that we
landed on within Q3 of 2016

00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:46.896
is what's called the
Coca Cola iconic endcap.

00:20:46.896 --> 00:20:48.020
I didn't give it that name.

00:20:48.020 --> 00:20:50.570
That's a pretty bold name
to give something when you

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:51.830
use the word iconic in there.

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:55.000
But that's the
company I work for.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:20:59.090
This endcap sits within
the grocery store

00:20:59.090 --> 00:21:00.770
sector and channel.

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:02.330
Allows you to walk up to it.

00:21:02.330 --> 00:21:06.170
And it shows you customized and
personalized content based off

00:21:06.170 --> 00:21:06.972
of who you are.

00:21:06.972 --> 00:21:08.930
The other thing that
we're able to do with that

00:21:08.930 --> 00:21:10.860
is bring in localized content.

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:13.040
So at my kid's
elementary school,

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:15.460
Coca Cola sponsors the
scoreboard that's there.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:19.040
They, frankly, did it before
my kid went to school there.

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:21.470
And when you're able to
take a picture of that,

00:21:21.470 --> 00:21:24.920
we're able to scrape social
media, bring in those imagery,

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:27.650
and show that in the
local supermarket.

00:21:27.650 --> 00:21:30.495
So even my son, when he goes
and scores a goal at soccer,

00:21:30.495 --> 00:21:32.870
the first thing he wants to
do is go to the grocery store

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:36.060
and see it on the iconic
endcap that's there.

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:38.060
But I get to talk to you
today about the Rev 2.0

00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:39.082
stuff that we've done.

00:21:39.082 --> 00:21:40.790
I'm really proud of
all these prototypes.

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:41.790
I think they're amazing.

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:43.880
And they produced amazing
results for the company.

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:45.546
But I'm absolutely
blown away about what

00:21:45.546 --> 00:21:47.640
we've been able to do now.

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:49.110
I am an architect.

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:51.310
I do write code, which
means any presentation

00:21:51.310 --> 00:21:54.130
that I give I am required
to show a block diagram.

00:21:54.130 --> 00:21:56.400
And that's exactly what
I'm going to do here now.

00:21:56.400 --> 00:21:57.870
We started out with Chrome.

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:00.380
And the reason why
we picked Chrome

00:22:00.380 --> 00:22:03.780
as an operating system for this
is not that it's easy to use,

00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:04.500
yes.

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:05.884
It's web standards based, yes.

00:22:05.884 --> 00:22:07.300
But really, at the
end of the day,

00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:08.790
it's the security
model of Chrome

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:10.310
that we were after, right?

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:12.750
Chrome has an amazing
security model

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:16.010
that keeps us rock
solid and rock secure,

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:17.760
especially within
our retail customers,

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:19.350
and retail environments.

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:20.976
And that's something
really important.

00:22:20.976 --> 00:22:23.100
When you start and look at
cyber-security and start

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:25.830
to realize that the average
take off a stolen credit card

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:28.334
number's about
$4.50 at this point,

00:22:28.334 --> 00:22:30.750
how much longer do you think
it is before we start hacking

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:31.380
coupons?

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:34.260
$0.50 Coke coupons,
and different things.

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:38.000
If a stolen credit card only
gets me $4.50 as a black hat,

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:40.650
it's not too much longer before
I'm going to go after Coke

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:42.060
coupons.

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:44.370
So start with Chrome
as an operating system.

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:46.130
Obviously we extend
in to the Chrome app,

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:47.713
and we go to the
screen at that point.

00:22:47.713 --> 00:22:50.644
That's a pretty simple device
design, at the end of the day.

00:22:50.644 --> 00:22:52.310
But then we start
getting fancy with it,

00:22:52.310 --> 00:22:53.849
and we start adding
into the Cloud.

00:22:53.849 --> 00:22:55.890
The first thing we did
was pretty straightforward

00:22:55.890 --> 00:22:57.000
and pretty simple.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:59.020
Looking at Chrome
Device Management.

00:22:59.020 --> 00:23:01.815
Now, Chrome Device Manager
brings back amazing diagnostics

00:23:01.815 --> 00:23:03.040
and analytics.

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:04.950
Things you get like
processor utilization,

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:06.320
memory utilization.

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:09.630
And it was invaluable to us
during this prototype process

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:12.240
as we were looking for memory
leaks and different things that

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:13.525
would keep it from running.

00:23:13.525 --> 00:23:15.150
But I think the
coolest thing about it,

00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:18.024
especially from a remote
management standpoint,

00:23:18.024 --> 00:23:19.440
is Chrome Device
Manager's ability

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:21.420
to bring back screenshots.

00:23:21.420 --> 00:23:24.630
I can't even begin to tell you
how useful that is when you're

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:29.070
flighting content to 500, 1,000,
1,500 screens at grocery stores

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:31.650
across the country,
and you want to make

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:33.784
sure the right
content is playing.

00:23:33.784 --> 00:23:35.700
There's nothing more
embarrassing than walking

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:38.116
into a grocery store in March
and seeing Christmas content

00:23:38.116 --> 00:23:39.750
on your displays, right?

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:41.970
And that ability with
Chrome Device Manager

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:43.810
lets us be able to do that.

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:45.650
The next piece that
we brought into this

00:23:45.650 --> 00:23:47.490
was Google Cloud, right?

00:23:47.490 --> 00:23:49.980
The power of the Cloud
to be able to drive

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:53.700
real-time targeting and
real-time personalization down

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:55.080
into that device.

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:58.410
That's what really gave us
that rich experience, what

00:23:58.410 --> 00:24:01.320
we like to call that Coca Cola
quality experience, that we

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:02.700
wanted to be able to drive.

00:24:02.700 --> 00:24:06.040
It's really that edition
of GCP into the middle.

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:09.340
The next thing we did though
was a little un-traditional.

00:24:09.340 --> 00:24:14.460
And we flight our content into
these systems with DoubleClick.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:17.384
Why would we use
DoubleClick, you might ask.

00:24:17.384 --> 00:24:19.050
Well, at the end of
the day, DoubleClick

00:24:19.050 --> 00:24:22.300
gives us a ton of advantages
that we're really looking for.

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:25.410
One of which is the targeting
engine inside of DoubleClick.

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:27.480
The next one that's in
there is the ability

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:29.290
to collaborate with people.

00:24:29.290 --> 00:24:31.170
And DoubleClick's
enormous ability

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:34.830
to be able to flight content,
do approvals for that content,

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:37.110
do brand management
for that content,

00:24:37.110 --> 00:24:39.810
and output the correct
analytical reports to all the

00:24:39.810 --> 00:24:41.290
stakeholders correctly.

00:24:41.290 --> 00:24:43.830
I kind of get on my soapbox
with Coke a little bit,

00:24:43.830 --> 00:24:46.380
sometimes when we
talk about content.

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:50.040
Coca Cola is a great company
that I'm very, very proud

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:52.560
to work for, but sometimes
I have to remind them

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:56.000
that we're not really a content
company, at the end of the day.

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:57.740
WebMD is a content company.

00:24:57.740 --> 00:25:00.150
Wikipedia is a content company.

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:03.392
What Coca Cola is doing is
called advertising, all right?

00:25:03.392 --> 00:25:05.100
When you're moving,
trying to get someone

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:06.930
to stop doing what
they're currently doing,

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:09.650
and do the thing that you want,
that is called advertising.

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:12.150
And when you have that level
of honesty around what you're

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:15.780
doing you're able to go get the
right tool for the right job.

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:18.047
And that's really what we
found with DoubleClick.

00:25:18.047 --> 00:25:20.130
One of the other enormous
benefits of DoubleClick,

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:21.755
which we'll show you
in a little while,

00:25:21.755 --> 00:25:23.280
is the analytical
capability of it.

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:24.990
The amount of data
we can get back

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:28.350
from these systems and
understanding who is actually

00:25:28.350 --> 00:25:30.720
standing in front of
our racks is absolutely

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:32.010
astounding to people.

00:25:35.210 --> 00:25:37.680
Screens are no fun if you
can't play with them, right?

00:25:37.680 --> 00:25:40.304
Who wants to just stand in front
of a screen and see something?

00:25:40.304 --> 00:25:42.650
So one of the other
big goals that we had

00:25:42.650 --> 00:25:45.570
was to be able to bring
in second string content.

00:25:45.570 --> 00:25:48.030
One of the other big goals
that we needed from a marketing

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:49.790
and ROI perspective
was the ability

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:52.550
to show conversion among
the investment, right?

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:55.650
So if I put a screen
in, what does it do?

00:25:55.650 --> 00:25:57.770
What benefit does it
bring to Coca Cola?

00:25:57.770 --> 00:26:00.300
What benefit does it
bring to the retailer?

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:03.430
And we found our
conversion with Eddystone.

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:06.230
Chrome devices all
have a Bluetooth radio.

00:26:06.230 --> 00:26:08.690
Google was nice enough to
post right up on GitHub

00:26:08.690 --> 00:26:11.660
how to turn any Chrome device
into an Eddystone beacon.

00:26:11.660 --> 00:26:13.140
And that's exactly what we did.

00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:16.160
We took and lifted the code
straight out of their examples

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:18.980
and put it into our design,
so that every single one

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:21.350
of those screens that's
broadcasting in there

00:26:21.350 --> 00:26:24.930
can broadcast Eddystone
content directly to the phone

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:27.140
without an app.

00:26:27.140 --> 00:26:29.920
We have become so
incredibly powerful.

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:34.130
In a world where we're so
used to app heavy engagements,

00:26:34.130 --> 00:26:38.270
being able just to simply
broadcast a URL over to a phone

00:26:38.270 --> 00:26:39.212
without an app.

00:26:39.212 --> 00:26:40.670
Now, on the other
side of the fence

00:26:40.670 --> 00:26:42.470
for places where
we did have an app,

00:26:42.470 --> 00:26:45.680
and we wanted to play
inside of a customer's app,

00:26:45.680 --> 00:26:47.540
we also found
Eddystone extremely

00:26:47.540 --> 00:26:50.780
useful in being able to
broadcast deep links directly

00:26:50.780 --> 00:26:53.270
into that app so that
I could direct people

00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:56.390
into that customer's app,
to the promotions page,

00:26:56.390 --> 00:26:58.880
to the Coke special page,
or wherever I actually

00:26:58.880 --> 00:27:00.770
needed them to broadcast to.

00:27:00.770 --> 00:27:03.560
And because all of this was
linked up with Google Cloud

00:27:03.560 --> 00:27:06.920
I could change every single
bit of that instantaneously

00:27:06.920 --> 00:27:09.860
on the fly to suit
whatever promotion,

00:27:09.860 --> 00:27:14.394
whatever event was
actually happening.

00:27:14.394 --> 00:27:16.310
But no solution is
complete without analytics.

00:27:16.310 --> 00:27:17.840
And I'm a huge numbers guy.

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:20.360
And I'm a huge believer
in data-driven marketing.

00:27:20.360 --> 00:27:24.650
So this is where it gets
super, super interesting.

00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:26.925
Being able to pull
data in from BigQuery

00:27:26.925 --> 00:27:29.300
into-- if you guys haven't
played with Google Data Studio

00:27:29.300 --> 00:27:31.910
yet, oh my god, what
an amazing product.

00:27:31.910 --> 00:27:34.010
It's almost like someone
went back to the 90s

00:27:34.010 --> 00:27:35.600
and found Crystal
Reports and made

00:27:35.600 --> 00:27:37.255
it actually work correctly.

00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:41.590
I'm serious.

00:27:41.590 --> 00:27:45.320
It's like, I wish
it was around then.

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:46.970
One of the other
things that we did

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.591
is, because we're Coca Cola,
and we work with so many people,

00:27:49.591 --> 00:27:50.090
right?

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:51.500
We have our customer.

00:27:51.500 --> 00:27:53.330
We have that customer's agency.

00:27:53.330 --> 00:27:55.610
We have that customer's
analytical agency.

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:57.770
We have this customer's
blah blah blah.

00:27:57.770 --> 00:28:00.815
We use Google Cloud as a
proxy for Google Analytics.

00:28:00.815 --> 00:28:02.690
And basically that means
that the information

00:28:02.690 --> 00:28:05.780
that we would normally be
broadcasting to DGA directly we

00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:06.860
send to the Cloud.

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:08.570
And then I can
spider it off into

00:28:08.570 --> 00:28:10.520
multiple analytical accounts.

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:12.530
So typically what
we'll do is we'll

00:28:12.530 --> 00:28:14.600
have one that the
customer is operating in,

00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:16.740
one the customer's
agency is operating in.

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:19.250
And then one that we might
be doing aggregate AB

00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:20.540
testing with for content.

00:28:20.540 --> 00:28:22.430
Things that we want
to keep to ourselves.

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:26.630
Or with my group, things are
extraordinarily experimental

00:28:26.630 --> 00:28:30.110
and might catch on
fire at any moment.

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:32.660
One of the great things
about using DoubleClick

00:28:32.660 --> 00:28:35.870
is the unification that happens
with the DoubleClick cookie.

00:28:35.870 --> 00:28:37.820
Now, we do this all
day long on the web.

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:40.040
Every single day today, right?

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:42.950
We use the DoubleClick cookie to
be able to unify our analytics

00:28:42.950 --> 00:28:44.960
and understand
who our users are.

00:28:44.960 --> 00:28:47.570
But we've never actually
been able to see this

00:28:47.570 --> 00:28:49.490
without spending an
enormous amount of money

00:28:49.490 --> 00:28:53.090
to agencies that provide this
information actually done

00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:54.540
in the physical world.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:57.230
And so what that means
is when you walk up

00:28:57.230 --> 00:28:59.570
to one of these racks, one
of these endcaps that I just

00:28:59.570 --> 00:29:01.700
showed you a picture of, right?

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:04.520
Without an email
address, without a login,

00:29:04.520 --> 00:29:06.590
without asking what
your Twitter handle is,

00:29:06.590 --> 00:29:09.110
or your Facebook handle,
or whatever social network

00:29:09.110 --> 00:29:13.910
login there is, we get real-time
information about who you are.

00:29:13.910 --> 00:29:14.840
Anonymous information.

00:29:14.840 --> 00:29:17.180
I don't really care what
your name is at that point.

00:29:17.180 --> 00:29:18.680
But I understand
your age bracket.

00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:19.730
I understand your sex.

00:29:19.730 --> 00:29:22.390
And I understand your
standard affinity categories.

00:29:22.390 --> 00:29:24.800
And what's really powerful
about these tools is even

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:27.110
with that information
I can turn around

00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:29.810
and do real-time
targeting against that.

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:32.660
And what we find with
that is that a lot

00:29:32.660 --> 00:29:34.370
of the misconceptions
that we had

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:36.710
about who our shopper
actually just got

00:29:36.710 --> 00:29:38.900
completely blown
out of the water.

00:29:38.900 --> 00:29:42.320
Even for a 130-year-old
company to really start

00:29:42.320 --> 00:29:47.380
to understand who exactly
is it doing the shopping.

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:50.590
So here is my required
block diagram.

00:29:50.590 --> 00:29:52.150
I'll be sending
a text to my boss

00:29:52.150 --> 00:29:53.580
and let her know that
this has been checked off

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:54.829
for my performance review now.

00:29:58.574 --> 00:29:59.074
Click.

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:11.505
Did you guys move over to the--

00:30:15.011 --> 00:30:15.510
there we go.

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:19.955
We advance the slide?

00:30:23.835 --> 00:30:27.230
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:27.230 --> 00:30:29.660
GREG CHAMBERS: Ha!

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:31.160
Technology demos
are like NASCAR.

00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:32.743
They're no fun until
somebody crashes.

00:30:40.020 --> 00:30:41.016
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:41.016 --> 00:30:42.510
GREG CHAMBERS: No.

00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:45.000
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:48.984
[INAUDIBLE]

00:30:48.984 --> 00:30:51.851
GREG CHAMBERS: Oh, here we go.

00:30:51.851 --> 00:30:52.350
Yeah.

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:52.920
There we go.

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:54.600
Thank you.

00:30:54.600 --> 00:30:58.580
Please come to every
presentation I do.

00:30:58.580 --> 00:31:01.352
So here's an
illustration of the cap.

00:31:01.352 --> 00:31:03.560
Now, here's the really great
thing about this endcap,

00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:05.950
and this really great thing
about being a part of Coke.

00:31:05.950 --> 00:31:09.210
This endcap actually
lifts all ships

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:11.020
on what's called the CSD aisle.

00:31:11.020 --> 00:31:13.320
For those of you who don't
work for a soda company,

00:31:13.320 --> 00:31:15.370
the CSD aisle is the
Carbonated Soft Drink

00:31:15.370 --> 00:31:17.740
aisle within the
grocery store, right?

00:31:17.740 --> 00:31:20.980
So not only did this endcap
produce a less than one

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:24.250
year ROI for us, all
right, it actually

00:31:24.250 --> 00:31:27.130
doubled that ROI for the
retailer in something

00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:29.650
that we call category lift.

00:31:29.650 --> 00:31:31.240
So it's one thing
when I approach

00:31:31.240 --> 00:31:33.979
a customer, like a grocery store
and say, hey, I'm Coca Cola,

00:31:33.979 --> 00:31:35.770
and I want to put an
end cap in your store.

00:31:35.770 --> 00:31:36.640
And they're like, great.

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:37.806
That's going to benefit you.

00:31:37.806 --> 00:31:39.100
How does it help me?

00:31:39.100 --> 00:31:40.980
At the end of the
day this endcap

00:31:40.980 --> 00:31:44.140
produces such a dramatic
category lift it's actually

00:31:44.140 --> 00:31:47.670
one month ROI in category left.

00:31:47.670 --> 00:31:51.160
And what that ultimately means
is not only did this endcap

00:31:51.160 --> 00:31:53.320
help sell more
Coca Cola products,

00:31:53.320 --> 00:31:57.400
it helped sell every other soda
product that was in that aisle

00:31:57.400 --> 00:32:01.660
at a rate that produced
a one month ROI.

00:32:01.660 --> 00:32:03.580
That's absolutely incredible.

00:32:03.580 --> 00:32:05.620
And it makes it a much
easier proposition

00:32:05.620 --> 00:32:08.090
when Coca Cola goes to
a retailer and says,

00:32:08.090 --> 00:32:10.570
hey, we'd like to do this,
to be able to tell what

00:32:10.570 --> 00:32:14.110
that value for you actually is.

00:32:14.110 --> 00:32:17.080
So at that point we felt
really, really confident

00:32:17.080 --> 00:32:20.590
that Coca Cola had a really,
really great digital signage

00:32:20.590 --> 00:32:21.662
solution, right?

00:32:21.662 --> 00:32:22.870
We were really happy with it.

00:32:22.870 --> 00:32:24.940
We were really
happy with the ROI.

00:32:24.940 --> 00:32:26.440
But I'll tell you,
one of the things

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:28.000
I love about working
at Coke is Coke

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:30.670
is really all about
bringing people together.

00:32:30.670 --> 00:32:31.810
That's not marketing fluff.

00:32:31.810 --> 00:32:33.226
That's really part
of our culture.

00:32:33.226 --> 00:32:36.040
We're southern guys, and
we're a southern company,

00:32:36.040 --> 00:32:37.540
and we're really
happy about when we

00:32:37.540 --> 00:32:39.130
get to bring people together.

00:32:39.130 --> 00:32:42.501
And so we didn't want to stop
with just a solution for us,

00:32:42.501 --> 00:32:43.000
right?

00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:44.830
What fun is that?

00:32:44.830 --> 00:32:46.420
We wanted to get
to the playground

00:32:46.420 --> 00:32:48.624
where we could actually
all play together.

00:32:48.624 --> 00:32:50.290
And that was one of
the big gaps that we

00:32:50.290 --> 00:32:54.160
saw in the marketplace was, how
do we get brands, consumers,

00:32:54.160 --> 00:32:58.090
and retailers to all play
together on the same playground

00:32:58.090 --> 00:33:01.590
and do so in an
incredibly effective way?

00:33:01.590 --> 00:33:04.480
And that's the next step
and the next iteration

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:06.620
that we took with us.

00:33:06.620 --> 00:33:08.520
So if you think about
digital endcaps, right?

00:33:08.520 --> 00:33:09.500
I'm a developer.

00:33:09.500 --> 00:33:10.687
I write code.

00:33:10.687 --> 00:33:12.520
And I think about things
in terms of classes

00:33:12.520 --> 00:33:14.080
and inheritance, right?

00:33:14.080 --> 00:33:17.680
So you think about digital
signage as a generic class.

00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:19.810
How do you inherit
down from that

00:33:19.810 --> 00:33:21.550
and start to get
to the specifics?

00:33:21.550 --> 00:33:23.380
And one of the next
big ones for Coca Cola,

00:33:23.380 --> 00:33:25.900
obviously, is
digital menu boards.

00:33:25.900 --> 00:33:27.740
You guys all know
digital menu boards.

00:33:27.740 --> 00:33:30.100
You walk into your favorite
quick serve restaurant

00:33:30.100 --> 00:33:32.427
and you see the TVs
up on the wall, right?

00:33:32.427 --> 00:33:33.760
And I know what you're thinking.

00:33:33.760 --> 00:33:34.720
You've probably
thought the same thing

00:33:34.720 --> 00:33:36.250
that I thought
when you saw them.

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:37.435
It's just for TVs.

00:33:37.435 --> 00:33:40.160
That system's pretty cheap.

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:41.110
Actually, no.

00:33:41.110 --> 00:33:42.420
Not so much.

00:33:42.420 --> 00:33:44.350
Gartner and Forrester
put the average cost

00:33:44.350 --> 00:33:48.940
of a four-panel digital menu
system at about $40,000,

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:49.510
all right?

00:33:49.510 --> 00:33:51.720
Now, I don't know if you've
been TV shopping lately,

00:33:51.720 --> 00:33:57.800
but four TVs and a Honda
Civic do not cost $40,000.

00:33:57.800 --> 00:34:00.270
Now, here's the other great
thing about digital menu boards

00:34:00.270 --> 00:34:01.120
to understand.

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:04.030
For some reason, and I'm still
wondering what this reason is,

00:34:04.030 --> 00:34:05.530
but I've seen the
research up, down,

00:34:05.530 --> 00:34:07.750
forwards, and backwards
to be able to see it,

00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:11.080
is digital menu boards drive
the sales of a restaurant,

00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:14.260
whether it's a multi-billion
dollar chain or a single mom

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:17.582
and pop, by about 4%, all right?

00:34:17.582 --> 00:34:19.040
Now, that may not
sound like a lot,

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:20.960
but it actually is
a lot, all right?

00:34:20.960 --> 00:34:26.920
That is a huge margin as
it comes into restaurants.

00:34:26.920 --> 00:34:29.830
So we, as Coca
Cola, wanted to be

00:34:29.830 --> 00:34:31.540
able to find a solution
that was cheaper

00:34:31.540 --> 00:34:34.929
on the marketplace, that
was better, more robust,

00:34:34.929 --> 00:34:37.989
and really had the features
that modern day restaurants were

00:34:37.989 --> 00:34:39.937
really looking for.

00:34:39.937 --> 00:34:41.770
And we found all the
help we needed in order

00:34:41.770 --> 00:34:45.230
to produce that with Google.

00:34:45.230 --> 00:34:48.139
So from a digital menu
board perspective,

00:34:48.139 --> 00:34:50.989
we've taken all the
intelligence, everything

00:34:50.989 --> 00:34:52.760
that we baked into
that Albertson's

00:34:52.760 --> 00:34:55.460
endcap, and that iconic
endcap, and built it

00:34:55.460 --> 00:34:57.800
into a digital
menu board product

00:34:57.800 --> 00:35:02.210
that we're now putting out into
the marketplace for about 10%

00:35:02.210 --> 00:35:04.610
of the cost of the
existing solutions.

00:35:04.610 --> 00:35:05.414
Why?

00:35:05.414 --> 00:35:06.830
Because we believe
all restaurants

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:09.380
should have all the
advantages of the big boys.

00:35:09.380 --> 00:35:11.180
We don't believe
that you should have

00:35:11.180 --> 00:35:13.340
to be the biggest
chain in the world

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:14.920
to have all the advantages.

00:35:14.920 --> 00:35:18.200
And we want to make sure that
every restaurant pours Coke,

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:21.830
of course, but also has every
advantage they could possibly

00:35:21.830 --> 00:35:24.110
have to be able to succeed.

00:35:24.110 --> 00:35:25.550
This digital menu
board system is

00:35:25.550 --> 00:35:26.960
designed in the
same architecture

00:35:26.960 --> 00:35:28.760
that I showed you in
the block diagram.

00:35:28.760 --> 00:35:31.150
And the only requirement
that we have with it--

00:35:31.150 --> 00:35:33.290
and you might be thinking, like,
OK, Coke, where is the catch?

00:35:33.290 --> 00:35:33.710
Like, right?

00:35:33.710 --> 00:35:34.490
There's going to be something.

00:35:34.490 --> 00:35:36.490
You're going to beam soda
into my mouth from it.

00:35:36.490 --> 00:35:39.859
Something weird is going
to happen here, right?

00:35:39.859 --> 00:35:41.900
The only catch is that
what we do is we make sure

00:35:41.900 --> 00:35:44.570
our products are represented
correctly on the screen.

00:35:44.570 --> 00:35:47.180
So we make sure that the
nutritional information

00:35:47.180 --> 00:35:49.700
is posted there so
consumers make educated

00:35:49.700 --> 00:35:51.260
choices about our products.

00:35:51.260 --> 00:35:53.960
We make sure that the latest
branding images are there.

00:35:53.960 --> 00:35:57.230
And the latest optimization
around how that digital menu

00:35:57.230 --> 00:35:58.640
board system works.

00:35:58.640 --> 00:36:02.450
For us, it's bringing back
the tradition of Coke signage,

00:36:02.450 --> 00:36:04.870
but in an amazing digital way.

00:36:04.870 --> 00:36:06.380
And we really have
Google to thank

00:36:06.380 --> 00:36:10.830
for helping us restore that
tradition to the company.

00:36:10.830 --> 00:36:12.840
So now let's get even
more interesting with it.

00:36:12.840 --> 00:36:16.120
So again, inheriting down
from digital signage.

00:36:16.120 --> 00:36:17.410
You think about posters.

00:36:17.410 --> 00:36:17.994
It's not much.

00:36:17.994 --> 00:36:19.160
I know what you're thinking.

00:36:19.160 --> 00:36:20.480
You know, fat guy on the stage.

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:21.650
He was talking about code.

00:36:21.650 --> 00:36:24.060
He probably doesn't like
movies at all, does he?

00:36:24.060 --> 00:36:25.380
You're going to be wrong there.

00:36:25.380 --> 00:36:27.737
So I love going to the cinema.

00:36:27.737 --> 00:36:30.320
There's nothing much more than
I love than going to the movies

00:36:30.320 --> 00:36:31.130
and having a Coke.

00:36:31.130 --> 00:36:32.750
But the number one
thing that drops me

00:36:32.750 --> 00:36:35.120
from being able to go to
the cinema is when I pull up

00:36:35.120 --> 00:36:37.040
and there's that
enormous line out front.

00:36:37.040 --> 00:36:40.050
Especially if it's raining
or something like that.

00:36:40.050 --> 00:36:42.020
So when we think about
the cinema and we

00:36:42.020 --> 00:36:44.780
think about all that
amazing real estate that's

00:36:44.780 --> 00:36:48.200
out front that's being used with
paper posters and light bulbs

00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:49.640
right now, right?

00:36:49.640 --> 00:36:52.550
What could we do with
that amazing real estate

00:36:52.550 --> 00:36:56.140
with the technology that we've
been able to create here?

00:36:56.140 --> 00:36:59.245
We've created that with what
we call the digital poster.

00:36:59.245 --> 00:37:02.680
All right, the digital poster
is a very simple application

00:37:02.680 --> 00:37:04.870
built on the same
technology that we just

00:37:04.870 --> 00:37:06.760
discussed in the block diagram.

00:37:06.760 --> 00:37:10.300
Allows you to walk up to
it, be able to purchase,

00:37:10.300 --> 00:37:13.630
understand this trailer,
understand show times,

00:37:13.630 --> 00:37:17.200
and purchase tickets for any
movie that's playing there.

00:37:17.200 --> 00:37:20.920
Further, With that DoubleClick
cookie and that customization

00:37:20.920 --> 00:37:23.290
that's there, that
poster is smart enough

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:25.840
to understand that
for the love of god,

00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:28.600
do not show me "Bridget
Jones' Baby," because I do not

00:37:28.600 --> 00:37:29.920
want to see that movie.

00:37:29.920 --> 00:37:31.840
That Greg is much
more interested

00:37:31.840 --> 00:37:34.300
in "Guardians of the
Galaxy" and "Rogue One"

00:37:34.300 --> 00:37:36.340
than he is even
"Hidden Figures."

00:37:36.340 --> 00:37:39.600
So we're able to show
people customized content.

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:41.650
We're able to show
them the actual things

00:37:41.650 --> 00:37:42.850
that they want to see.

00:37:42.850 --> 00:37:44.770
And take all that
benefit that we're

00:37:44.770 --> 00:37:47.650
used to online from
e-commerce and bring

00:37:47.650 --> 00:37:49.615
it directly to
the physical world

00:37:49.615 --> 00:37:51.490
where you can touch it,
and feel it, and have

00:37:51.490 --> 00:37:55.120
the tactical sensation
of it in your hands.

00:37:55.120 --> 00:37:57.520
But wait, there's more.

00:37:57.520 --> 00:38:00.790
Number one thing that I enjoy at
a movie is popcorn and a Coke.

00:38:00.790 --> 00:38:03.850
But again, the
concession line inside.

00:38:03.850 --> 00:38:06.040
And I know from
personal experience

00:38:06.040 --> 00:38:07.510
and my own
experiences, but I also

00:38:07.510 --> 00:38:09.516
have research to back
this up, because we're

00:38:09.516 --> 00:38:11.140
the kind of company
that funds research

00:38:11.140 --> 00:38:14.430
like this, that has kind
of like duh moments to it,

00:38:14.430 --> 00:38:16.180
but when the line's
long at the concession

00:38:16.180 --> 00:38:19.750
stand people don't go
to the concession stand.

00:38:19.750 --> 00:38:22.060
So ultimately within
that same thing,

00:38:22.060 --> 00:38:24.020
using the magic of
Google Cloud, and being

00:38:24.020 --> 00:38:26.720
able to do simple web
hooks inside of there,

00:38:26.720 --> 00:38:29.650
we're able to broadcast
in and bring the ability

00:38:29.650 --> 00:38:32.980
to order your Coke and popcorn
and have it sent directly

00:38:32.980 --> 00:38:34.750
over to the POS of
the concession stand

00:38:34.750 --> 00:38:37.150
where you pick it up
with no wait, which means

00:38:37.150 --> 00:38:38.800
I can go to the movies now.

00:38:38.800 --> 00:38:40.540
I don't wait in line out front.

00:38:40.540 --> 00:38:42.790
I don't wait in line
for my Coke and popcorn.

00:38:42.790 --> 00:38:44.830
I don't have to remember
to do it ahead of time.

00:38:44.830 --> 00:38:46.630
I don't have to do
that mad scramble

00:38:46.630 --> 00:38:49.420
on my phone in the parking lot
to be able to purchase tickets.

00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:52.540
I walk up like a leisurely
human being that I am,

00:38:52.540 --> 00:38:54.640
be able to walk
up to any poster,

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:56.500
be able to execute
my transaction,

00:38:56.500 --> 00:38:59.326
and enjoy my experience.

00:38:59.326 --> 00:39:00.700
This is something
really powerful

00:39:00.700 --> 00:39:02.950
we're really, really
proud of, and we'll

00:39:02.950 --> 00:39:05.320
be unveiling more of this
at Cinemacon coming up

00:39:05.320 --> 00:39:07.030
this month in Las Vegas.

00:39:09.540 --> 00:39:10.730
So let's get to analytics.

00:39:10.730 --> 00:39:14.510
This is where it gets
super interesting.

00:39:14.510 --> 00:39:15.380
I love my brand.

00:39:15.380 --> 00:39:16.130
I love my company.

00:39:16.130 --> 00:39:18.170
But I also like to pick
on them a little bit.

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:20.840
My company is one of
those companies that

00:39:20.840 --> 00:39:22.300
thinks they know everything.

00:39:22.300 --> 00:39:23.830
We're a 130-year-old company.

00:39:23.830 --> 00:39:27.410
We know exactly who
our shopper is, right?

00:39:27.410 --> 00:39:29.445
The power of these
analytics that we get back

00:39:29.445 --> 00:39:31.070
from that DoubleClick
cookie, and being

00:39:31.070 --> 00:39:32.480
able to serve
content from there,

00:39:32.480 --> 00:39:35.060
are incredible, all right?

00:39:35.060 --> 00:39:37.880
This is actual analytical
data that we mocked up

00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:41.300
for this screen from an actual
Google Analytics account.

00:39:41.300 --> 00:39:46.400
And my marketers swore to me
that the number one demographic

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:48.920
that was engaging within
this retail environment

00:39:48.920 --> 00:39:52.940
where this data's from was
a boomer generation mom.

00:39:52.940 --> 00:39:54.200
Swore to me.

00:39:54.200 --> 00:39:57.055
On their life, swore to it.

00:39:57.055 --> 00:39:58.880
You know what we
came out of here?

00:39:58.880 --> 00:40:01.110
Millennial males.

00:40:01.110 --> 00:40:02.280
Kid you not.

00:40:02.280 --> 00:40:06.420
We print up all our marketing
material completely focused

00:40:06.420 --> 00:40:08.730
at the wrong shopper.

00:40:08.730 --> 00:40:10.710
Analytical data
gives us this ability

00:40:10.710 --> 00:40:13.110
to be able to understand
this in real time.

00:40:13.110 --> 00:40:16.590
Further, we get the ability
with native Google tools,

00:40:16.590 --> 00:40:19.560
stuff that's out there
today, to be able to target

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:21.270
to those people directly.

00:40:21.270 --> 00:40:24.180
So that I can send different
content for the millennial,

00:40:24.180 --> 00:40:27.780
to the boomer generation,
to male, to female, and even

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:29.910
into the affinity
categories where

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:32.370
I can do real-time AB
testing to understand

00:40:32.370 --> 00:40:34.367
the validity of my content.

00:40:34.367 --> 00:40:35.950
Now, something really
great about Coke

00:40:35.950 --> 00:40:38.960
is we learn an awful lot
from our campaigns, right?

00:40:38.960 --> 00:40:40.500
And I think everyone
knows Share A

00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:43.440
Coke is one of our great
pillar summer campaigns.

00:40:43.440 --> 00:40:44.430
Huge fan of it.

00:40:44.430 --> 00:40:45.930
I think it's amazing.

00:40:45.930 --> 00:40:48.570
But at the end of the day,
being able to understand

00:40:48.570 --> 00:40:50.850
our consumers like
this means that we

00:40:50.850 --> 00:40:53.580
start to flop our learnings
on a weekly basis,

00:40:53.580 --> 00:40:55.260
instead of a seasonal basis.

00:40:55.260 --> 00:40:58.000
So everything that we learn
from a campaign like Share A

00:40:58.000 --> 00:41:00.360
Coke, that might run for
three or four months,

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:04.080
we're now able to learn those
kinds of things in a week,

00:41:04.080 --> 00:41:07.590
and start to flip it
over faster, and faster,

00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:10.230
and faster, which means
our marketers get smarter,

00:41:10.230 --> 00:41:13.260
our technology get smarter,
and our products ultimately

00:41:13.260 --> 00:41:17.880
get to refresh more
people that way.

00:41:17.880 --> 00:41:20.940
So one of my big speeches
that I really love

00:41:20.940 --> 00:41:24.480
is I love this reconverging
of e-commerce and commerce.

00:41:24.480 --> 00:41:27.090
For the longest time we had
these two separate tracks

00:41:27.090 --> 00:41:30.480
that were like cats and dogs
that didn't get along, right?

00:41:30.480 --> 00:41:32.925
So much so they even had to
have their own sale days.

00:41:32.925 --> 00:41:34.830
They're separated by a weekend.

00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:37.350
You know, the commerce
world had Black Friday.

00:41:37.350 --> 00:41:39.611
The e-commerce world
had Cyber Monday.

00:41:39.611 --> 00:41:41.610
So at the end of the day,
what we really believe

00:41:41.610 --> 00:41:43.260
is that these worlds
are converging.

00:41:43.260 --> 00:41:44.970
Now, the research supports this.

00:41:44.970 --> 00:41:48.030
Generation Z is much,
much more into being

00:41:48.030 --> 00:41:51.120
able to shop in store
than they are online.

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:53.869
And everybody has their own
affinity when it comes to this.

00:41:53.869 --> 00:41:56.160
But we also really believe
with these tools from Google

00:41:56.160 --> 00:41:57.810
we're able to merge
these worlds and we

00:41:57.810 --> 00:42:01.370
can get back to just
plain old commerce again.

00:42:01.370 --> 00:42:04.927
And we don't have to have
these two separate tracks that

00:42:04.927 --> 00:42:06.010
are running through there.

00:42:08.700 --> 00:42:11.130
This is the big one that I'm
really, really proud about.

00:42:11.130 --> 00:42:13.500
We've taken all of this
information that we've done.

00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:16.230
And we're compiling this into
a product called Google Retail

00:42:16.230 --> 00:42:17.130
Cloud.

00:42:17.130 --> 00:42:20.520
And ultimately what that means
is all these fragmented sensors

00:42:20.520 --> 00:42:23.130
that are out there, all these
amazing technologies that all

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:25.490
these ecosystem providers
are being able to do,

00:42:25.490 --> 00:42:28.830
is we built a simple environment
for all of these things

00:42:28.830 --> 00:42:30.720
to be able to interact
with together.

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:33.660
A simple environment to take
the output of your beacons,

00:42:33.660 --> 00:42:37.850
or your thermocouple sensors,
or even your LIDAR sensor,

00:42:37.850 --> 00:42:40.050
and be able to tie
that directly back

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:42.539
to signage in an
easy automated way.

00:42:42.539 --> 00:42:44.580
You know, we're Coca Cola,
and with the exception

00:42:44.580 --> 00:42:46.800
of a few exceptions in the
world, like the Las Vegas

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:49.080
strip, and our World of
Coca Cola in Atlanta,

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:51.960
we don't really have
a Coke store, right?

00:42:51.960 --> 00:42:56.460
We sell our products inside
of everybody else's store.

00:42:56.460 --> 00:42:59.430
So when you think about
fractured retail environments

00:42:59.430 --> 00:43:01.500
and fractured retail
technology, this

00:43:01.500 --> 00:43:04.540
is a problem that
overwhelms us at Coca Cola.

00:43:04.540 --> 00:43:06.870
We're Coca Cola,
we're sold everywhere,

00:43:06.870 --> 00:43:09.810
which means I've got to work
with everybody's technology

00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:10.539
stack.

00:43:10.539 --> 00:43:13.080
And there are people out there
still running like Windows 3.1

00:43:13.080 --> 00:43:15.520
on this stuff,
believe it or not.

00:43:15.520 --> 00:43:17.910
But what Google Retail Cloud
gives us the ability to do

00:43:17.910 --> 00:43:20.700
is to have a common
playground for these things.

00:43:20.700 --> 00:43:23.490
A common place for brands
to be able to flight

00:43:23.490 --> 00:43:27.150
this content, that makes it a
brand-friendly environment so

00:43:27.150 --> 00:43:30.900
that Coca Cola, Clorox,
Campbell's Soup,

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:33.390
all of these guys can
flight their content

00:43:33.390 --> 00:43:36.720
into a common safe
repository, which

00:43:36.720 --> 00:43:40.200
means that all the content
being served down to consumers

00:43:40.200 --> 00:43:43.710
can be verified from a single
domain, which means my cyber

00:43:43.710 --> 00:43:46.770
security concerns start
to melt away a little bit.

00:43:46.770 --> 00:43:48.990
Cyber security concerns
never go away completely,

00:43:48.990 --> 00:43:51.077
but they melt away a little bit.

00:43:51.077 --> 00:43:53.160
And I don't get so concerned
that my consumers are

00:43:53.160 --> 00:43:54.902
going to have a bad experience.

00:43:54.902 --> 00:43:56.610
The other thing that
we're able to create

00:43:56.610 --> 00:44:00.060
is truly dynamic and truly
personalized environments.

00:44:00.060 --> 00:44:02.310
By standardizing the
technology layer that

00:44:02.310 --> 00:44:04.290
sits within the
retail store I can

00:44:04.290 --> 00:44:06.570
take all these amazing
hardware innovations,

00:44:06.570 --> 00:44:09.120
all these amazing things
that people are producing

00:44:09.120 --> 00:44:10.740
and actually have
them in a place

00:44:10.740 --> 00:44:13.542
where I can utilize them
to their full potential.

00:44:13.542 --> 00:44:15.375
There's nothing that's
disappointing to Coca

00:44:15.375 --> 00:44:17.850
Cola like somebody
taking a good technology,

00:44:17.850 --> 00:44:21.120
putting it in their store in
a bad implementation, right?

00:44:21.120 --> 00:44:23.070
You guys, you're all
technologists here

00:44:23.070 --> 00:44:23.700
at Google Next.

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:25.658
You all understand this
the same way that I do.

00:44:25.658 --> 00:44:28.110
You see the tech, and you
know the value of the tech,

00:44:28.110 --> 00:44:29.568
and you look at
the implementation,

00:44:29.568 --> 00:44:33.417
and you're like, oh man, why
did you guys do it like that?

00:44:33.417 --> 00:44:35.250
Ultimately what Google
Retail Cloud gives us

00:44:35.250 --> 00:44:38.280
the ability is to have that
standardized environment so

00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:41.730
that we can be able to flight
our content, flight our offers,

00:44:41.730 --> 00:44:44.490
do things that take the
technology hurdle out

00:44:44.490 --> 00:44:47.280
of the way and get back to
doing more of what we love,

00:44:47.280 --> 00:44:50.610
surprise and delight,
experiences that enlighten

00:44:50.610 --> 00:44:52.870
and refresh our consumers.

00:44:52.870 --> 00:44:55.990
The next thing, obviously
we are at Google Next.

00:44:55.990 --> 00:44:57.700
That cloud is Google-powered.

00:44:57.700 --> 00:44:59.560
There's no other
company out there

00:44:59.560 --> 00:45:02.770
in the world who produces
the kind of cloud technology

00:45:02.770 --> 00:45:04.000
that Google does.

00:45:04.000 --> 00:45:05.980
I promise you I'm
the kind of architect

00:45:05.980 --> 00:45:09.470
who doesn't believe most the
time in using one vendor,

00:45:09.470 --> 00:45:10.060
right?

00:45:10.060 --> 00:45:12.010
I've never been
the guy that took

00:45:12.010 --> 00:45:15.920
the entire suite from a
company and be able to use it.

00:45:15.920 --> 00:45:20.410
And we didn't set out to
blow smoke up Google's skirt

00:45:20.410 --> 00:45:22.060
to be able to build
it in this way.

00:45:22.060 --> 00:45:25.030
We set out to build it with
the best tools and the best

00:45:25.030 --> 00:45:26.980
components on the marketplace.

00:45:26.980 --> 00:45:30.850
And that just ended up happening
to be the Google tools.

00:45:30.850 --> 00:45:33.880
We never really wanted it
to be all from one company,

00:45:33.880 --> 00:45:35.050
at the end of the day.

00:45:35.050 --> 00:45:37.360
But what we found
time and time again

00:45:37.360 --> 00:45:40.300
as we looked at each individual
component of the solution

00:45:40.300 --> 00:45:43.060
was that Google made the best
powered products that really

00:45:43.060 --> 00:45:46.690
made the situation work for us.

00:45:46.690 --> 00:45:48.620
So getting to my playground.

00:45:48.620 --> 00:45:51.110
Getting to the playground
where we can all play together.

00:45:51.110 --> 00:45:53.720
An ecosystem is no
fun by yourself.

00:45:53.720 --> 00:45:55.700
So we've lined up
some amazing partners

00:45:55.700 --> 00:45:57.750
to be able to work
within this ecosystem.

00:45:57.750 --> 00:45:59.666
It's not just Coke's
ecosystem, right?

00:45:59.666 --> 00:46:01.790
What we want is we just
want the ability to refresh

00:46:01.790 --> 00:46:04.089
our consumers at the
end of the day, right?

00:46:04.089 --> 00:46:06.380
And we want to leave the
heavy lifting around hardware,

00:46:06.380 --> 00:46:09.020
and software, and
Cloud to the experts.

00:46:09.020 --> 00:46:12.960
Like NEC, Aopen, and
LG, as well as Google,

00:46:12.960 --> 00:46:15.890
so that we can have real tools
that are available out there.

00:46:15.890 --> 00:46:19.010
Displays that are custom
built for these environments.

00:46:19.010 --> 00:46:22.040
Menu board products that
go up with four screws.

00:46:22.040 --> 00:46:24.620
Things that we can
easily start to change.

00:46:24.620 --> 00:46:27.230
One of the things that has me
the most excited about this

00:46:27.230 --> 00:46:29.810
is that a lot of implementations
of retail technology

00:46:29.810 --> 00:46:32.210
that we see at Coca
Cola, it's really hard

00:46:32.210 --> 00:46:34.490
to figure out what
you should put where.

00:46:34.490 --> 00:46:36.930
So what we end up doing is
kind of watering down the tech

00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:40.190
mix to make a generic to
work across the entire United

00:46:40.190 --> 00:46:43.730
States, or even in my sector,
the entire globe, right?

00:46:43.730 --> 00:46:45.710
What we really have the
ability to do with this

00:46:45.710 --> 00:46:47.660
is customize the
mix of technology

00:46:47.660 --> 00:46:49.289
to each individual environment.

00:46:49.289 --> 00:46:51.830
Because I'll tell you, I don't
know how much you guys travel,

00:46:51.830 --> 00:46:54.080
but a store is kind of
different in Nebraska

00:46:54.080 --> 00:46:55.802
than it is downtown Manhattan.

00:46:55.802 --> 00:46:57.260
That's not necessarily
a bad thing.

00:46:57.260 --> 00:46:59.130
Doesn't mean that one's
better than the other.

00:46:59.130 --> 00:47:00.421
But they're a little different.

00:47:00.421 --> 00:47:02.000
And they're different
in California

00:47:02.000 --> 00:47:04.140
than they are New Zealand,
than they are Australia,

00:47:04.140 --> 00:47:06.980
than they are Western Europe,
than they are Asia Pacific.

00:47:06.980 --> 00:47:09.080
So this really gives
the ability not

00:47:09.080 --> 00:47:11.840
to have to have that
one technology solution,

00:47:11.840 --> 00:47:15.290
that one mix that everything
goes into there with.

00:47:15.290 --> 00:47:19.520
Now, here's the other thing
about this revolution that's

00:47:19.520 --> 00:47:20.219
coming.

00:47:20.219 --> 00:47:22.010
It's going to take a
lot of wrench turners.

00:47:22.010 --> 00:47:24.200
You got a lot of
screens that need mount.

00:47:24.200 --> 00:47:25.790
You've got a lot
of service provider

00:47:25.790 --> 00:47:27.998
work that needs to go into
there to be able to do it.

00:47:27.998 --> 00:47:30.860
And we've lined up some great
partners for that as well.

00:47:30.860 --> 00:47:33.470
One of which I want to
give a really big shout out

00:47:33.470 --> 00:47:34.784
to Radius Networks.

00:47:34.784 --> 00:47:36.950
They've been a partner of
ours since pretty much day

00:47:36.950 --> 00:47:39.230
one in this, and I've
been amazing sports

00:47:39.230 --> 00:47:41.750
as we went down this journey,
as we went through especially,

00:47:41.750 --> 00:47:45.336
some of the early alpha
prototypes that, you know,

00:47:45.336 --> 00:47:47.235
it's fine if they're alphas.

00:47:47.235 --> 00:47:48.110
They did what alphas.

00:47:48.110 --> 00:47:49.080
Are supposed to do.

00:47:49.080 --> 00:47:51.840
So I want to give a big shout
out to Mark Wallace and Radius

00:47:51.840 --> 00:47:55.310
Networks for helping us there.

00:47:55.310 --> 00:47:57.800
Within that ecosystem we also
have another great partner

00:47:57.800 --> 00:47:59.345
that we've used in Process.

00:47:59.345 --> 00:48:01.727
And [INAUDIBLE] in PROCESS
has been absolutely

00:48:01.727 --> 00:48:04.310
amazing in being able to help
us realize a lot of these dreams

00:48:04.310 --> 00:48:05.726
and get this stuff
off the ground.

00:48:07.929 --> 00:48:09.970
Within there, the next
piece that everybody needs

00:48:09.970 --> 00:48:11.920
is creative agencies.

00:48:11.920 --> 00:48:14.200
And we've lined up some
amazing creative agencies here

00:48:14.200 --> 00:48:18.190
to be able to see, flight
content directly to this.

00:48:18.190 --> 00:48:20.530
But here's another great
thing about this, though.

00:48:20.530 --> 00:48:23.230
We're at Coca Cola, I
work with literally, this

00:48:23.230 --> 00:48:25.760
is not a made-up number
or hyperbole here,

00:48:25.760 --> 00:48:29.310
200 different creative agencies.

00:48:29.310 --> 00:48:31.840
200, all right?

00:48:31.840 --> 00:48:33.880
You know what's great
about 200 agencies?

00:48:33.880 --> 00:48:36.580
Is every single
one of them already

00:48:36.580 --> 00:48:38.830
knows how to flight content
to double DoubleClick.

00:48:38.830 --> 00:48:40.750
Every single one of them.

00:48:40.750 --> 00:48:42.160
No learning curve.

00:48:42.160 --> 00:48:43.240
No training.

00:48:43.240 --> 00:48:44.470
No videos.

00:48:44.470 --> 00:48:45.490
No nothing.

00:48:45.490 --> 00:48:48.310
They already know how to use it.

00:48:48.310 --> 00:48:51.460
That's a huge advantage to us.

00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:54.670
So that's a little bit of
the story around how we

00:48:54.670 --> 00:48:56.567
got to this point to do this.

00:48:56.567 --> 00:48:58.900
But let's get hands-on with
it, and let's go to the demo

00:48:58.900 --> 00:49:02.380
and actually see this in action
so we can see it work and play.

00:49:02.380 --> 00:49:05.410
So here we have
our lovely, I think

00:49:05.410 --> 00:49:08.530
we named this Burger
Barn, real creative name.

00:49:08.530 --> 00:49:12.970
The three-panel menu
board that we're

00:49:12.970 --> 00:49:14.719
able to put our products within.

00:49:14.719 --> 00:49:16.510
Now, you have a couple
of different choices

00:49:16.510 --> 00:49:18.010
in how you do this.

00:49:18.010 --> 00:49:20.560
You can actually layer
these in as single images

00:49:20.560 --> 00:49:22.960
if you want to design them
in a tool like Photoshop.

00:49:22.960 --> 00:49:24.850
Or you can layer
them in a individuals

00:49:24.850 --> 00:49:26.140
that come within there.

00:49:26.140 --> 00:49:30.400
Now, we do a lot of research at
Coca Cola, a lot of research.

00:49:30.400 --> 00:49:33.820
Like, we have actually spun
up an entire million dollar

00:49:33.820 --> 00:49:37.240
research stream around
what's called the twinkle.

00:49:37.240 --> 00:49:41.740
The twinkle is how that product
actually displays itself

00:49:41.740 --> 00:49:43.174
to on that menu board.

00:49:43.174 --> 00:49:45.340
And this product that we've
developed in conjunction

00:49:45.340 --> 00:49:47.590
with Google that displays
them is ultimately

00:49:47.590 --> 00:49:50.350
able to take advantage
of all of that research.

00:49:50.350 --> 00:49:54.405
So layering in, this is just
simply the output of it.

00:49:54.405 --> 00:49:55.488
Let's look at the backend.

00:49:58.360 --> 00:50:00.330
The backend is
very, very simple.

00:50:00.330 --> 00:50:02.160
Allows you to
manage all of this.

00:50:02.160 --> 00:50:04.590
This is all hosted
inside of Google Cloud.

00:50:04.590 --> 00:50:06.240
So it's all cloud-based.

00:50:06.240 --> 00:50:08.100
It's all extremely responsive.

00:50:08.100 --> 00:50:10.930
And it was designed and built
with Google Material Design.

00:50:10.930 --> 00:50:13.170
So at the end of the
day you're able to group

00:50:13.170 --> 00:50:15.060
your displays into
various groups,

00:50:15.060 --> 00:50:16.890
however you want to group them.

00:50:16.890 --> 00:50:19.030
That can be by department
within a grocery store.

00:50:19.030 --> 00:50:22.080
It can be by store if you're
a multi-source portfolio.

00:50:22.080 --> 00:50:26.399
It can also be however you want
to put those things together

00:50:26.399 --> 00:50:28.440
and be able to group them
together from a content

00:50:28.440 --> 00:50:30.120
perspective.

00:50:30.120 --> 00:50:32.110
The next thing that
we're able to look at

00:50:32.110 --> 00:50:34.157
is how to configure schedules.

00:50:34.157 --> 00:50:36.240
Now, anyone who's been in
a food service or retail

00:50:36.240 --> 00:50:38.130
for more than five
minutes understands

00:50:38.130 --> 00:50:41.460
the power and the benefit
of dayparting, right?

00:50:41.460 --> 00:50:43.860
You don't want to show consumers
the same advertisements

00:50:43.860 --> 00:50:46.401
in the morning that you're going
to show them in the evening.

00:50:46.401 --> 00:50:48.640
For Coca Cola that's
a very simple thing.

00:50:48.640 --> 00:50:50.610
We advertise juice
in the morning.

00:50:50.610 --> 00:50:53.460
We advertise carbonated soft
drinks in the afternoon.

00:50:53.460 --> 00:50:56.520
For different retailers, this
varies all over the place.

00:50:56.520 --> 00:50:59.550
This system allows you to be
able to do that scheduling,

00:50:59.550 --> 00:51:03.150
and that dayparting
very easily, very simply

00:51:03.150 --> 00:51:04.734
and migrate your screens around.

00:51:04.734 --> 00:51:06.900
The other thing that I
really love about this system

00:51:06.900 --> 00:51:10.260
too is the ability to
repurpose screens, right?

00:51:10.260 --> 00:51:13.560
When you think of digital menu
boards today, they're static.

00:51:13.560 --> 00:51:14.554
They might be digital.

00:51:14.554 --> 00:51:15.720
They might have the twinkle.

00:51:15.720 --> 00:51:17.650
They might have all the fun
graphics and everything.

00:51:17.650 --> 00:51:18.566
But it's a menu board.

00:51:18.566 --> 00:51:20.250
And that's what it does, right?

00:51:20.250 --> 00:51:22.140
But think about the
cinema example, again.

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:25.560
You have certain days within
the cinema, like, I don't know,

00:51:25.560 --> 00:51:27.720
the day the new Star
Wars movie comes out,

00:51:27.720 --> 00:51:30.000
it's a lot busier than others.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:31.500
What you have the
ability to do is

00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:35.190
instantly and on the fly
repurpose screens with this.

00:51:35.190 --> 00:51:38.580
So you can take a screen,
plug a Chrome device into it,

00:51:38.580 --> 00:51:40.780
and on Monday it can be
a digital menu board,

00:51:40.780 --> 00:51:42.644
on Tuesday it can
be a digital poster.

00:51:42.644 --> 00:51:44.310
And you have the
ability to repurpose it

00:51:44.310 --> 00:51:47.930
as that individual retail
environment sees fit.

00:51:47.930 --> 00:51:51.260
That, to us, is a really,
really powerful feature of this.

00:51:51.260 --> 00:51:55.290
And not having screens that
get locked individually.

00:51:55.290 --> 00:51:57.612
Looking at the
different layouts we

00:51:57.612 --> 00:51:59.070
have several layouts
that we've put

00:51:59.070 --> 00:52:00.720
in the initial release of this.

00:52:00.720 --> 00:52:02.580
But this is also
extensible where

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:05.254
we can start to add new
multiple layouts with it.

00:52:05.254 --> 00:52:06.670
And this is where
Coca Cola starts

00:52:06.670 --> 00:52:09.360
to contribute to this, is we do
our shopper marketing research.

00:52:09.360 --> 00:52:11.700
We do our focus group
research to understand

00:52:11.700 --> 00:52:14.460
which one of these layouts
is actually moving product

00:52:14.460 --> 00:52:15.810
at the end of the day.

00:52:15.810 --> 00:52:18.900
Now, there's visual layouts that
are done for visual aesthetic.

00:52:18.900 --> 00:52:21.060
There's also layouts within
the retail environment

00:52:21.060 --> 00:52:23.490
that are optimized to
actually move product.

00:52:23.490 --> 00:52:26.059
And that's where you start to
see things like that 4% lift

00:52:26.059 --> 00:52:26.850
and I talked about.

00:52:29.530 --> 00:52:32.050
The next thing that we're able
to click through into here

00:52:32.050 --> 00:52:34.630
is actually being able to
see that content actually

00:52:34.630 --> 00:52:36.640
move in real time.

00:52:36.640 --> 00:52:38.620
This becomes immensely
powerful in being

00:52:38.620 --> 00:52:40.660
able to understand
exactly what this is going

00:52:40.660 --> 00:52:43.030
to look like at any given time.

00:52:43.030 --> 00:52:45.287
And then also helps
you move back and forth

00:52:45.287 --> 00:52:47.620
between those environments
of using it as a digital menu

00:52:47.620 --> 00:52:51.220
board, or as a poster, or
other various different aspects

00:52:51.220 --> 00:52:52.950
of it.

00:52:52.950 --> 00:52:54.990
The final thing that I
wanted to show you guys

00:52:54.990 --> 00:52:56.220
is the analytics from this.

00:52:56.220 --> 00:52:59.130
This is the thing that
has me the most excited

00:52:59.130 --> 00:53:01.170
from a Coca Cola perspective.

00:53:01.170 --> 00:53:04.110
I won't name them, but
we spend a lot of money

00:53:04.110 --> 00:53:06.431
with these research
agencies that

00:53:06.431 --> 00:53:08.430
are supposed to be supplying
us with information

00:53:08.430 --> 00:53:10.260
about who our consumer is.

00:53:10.260 --> 00:53:13.710
And honestly, what we see
with DoubleClick and Google

00:53:13.710 --> 00:53:16.720
Analytics completely
dust those guys.

00:53:16.720 --> 00:53:18.150
We are able to--

00:53:18.150 --> 00:53:21.450
at zero cost, I might
add, except for the fees

00:53:21.450 --> 00:53:24.120
that we pay to DoubleClick,
and the peanuts

00:53:24.120 --> 00:53:26.660
that we end up paying
to Google Cloud--

00:53:26.660 --> 00:53:30.090
we're able to understand
exactly who our consumer is.

00:53:30.090 --> 00:53:32.830
We're able to understand
age brackets, sex,

00:53:32.830 --> 00:53:35.340
and male female
splits within there.

00:53:35.340 --> 00:53:38.520
That helps us get the right
content, and the right message,

00:53:38.520 --> 00:53:41.070
to the right person,
at the right time.

00:53:41.070 --> 00:53:43.210
And that provides
utility to people.

00:53:43.210 --> 00:53:45.420
Not only does it help
us sell our product.

00:53:45.420 --> 00:53:48.270
It helps us drive an
amazing experience,

00:53:48.270 --> 00:53:50.910
whether that happens within
a retail environment, a food

00:53:50.910 --> 00:53:53.490
service environment, or
even on-premise locations

00:53:53.490 --> 00:53:56.460
like universities,
or theme parks.

